abilities.fireCode:
// Halve the dish's pull for three seconds. Nothing else changes, so the
// drive that was already carrying it outward now wins against a bowl
// that is only half there and it hangs on the lip instead of sliding
// back down. It also sits in the charge band the whole time, which is
// the reward, and inside exitNeed the whole time, which is the bill.
a.tether = a.t + 3.0; break;

// Shed's opposite. Shed throws the counterweights off and loses the
// mass with them; Scatter walks them round the ring until they cancel
// and keeps every gram. The divisors read a.spec.imb, which is still the
// OLD imbalance while the object literal is being evaluated, so drive
// and stamina land back exactly where they started before any weight
// was fitted. Do not reorder these two lines.
a.spec = Object.assign({}, a.spec, {
  imb: 0, imbAng: 0,
  drive: a.spec.drive / (1 + K.imbDrive * a.spec.imb),
  stamina: a.spec.stamina / (1 - 0.42 * a.spec.imb)
});
break;

// A two second window where the rim actually grips. Two levers, and the
// second one is the one that works: `gear` sets how hard it grabs, and
// the jt CEILING in collide sets how much of that grab is allowed to
// land. See the collide hook, because gear alone is provably nothing.
a.bite = a.t + 2.0; break;

abilities.collideHook:
1. The gear multipliers (currently lines 807 and 808) are already correct and
   stay as they are:

    const gearA = a.spec.gear * (a.t < a.bite ? 3 : 1) * (a.spec.dw && a.spec.dw.hungry ? 1.40 : 1);
    const gearB = b.spec.gear * (b.t < b.bite ? 3 : 1) * (b.spec.dw && b.spec.dw.hungry ? 1.40 : 1);

2. REQUIRED. Replace the jtCap line (currently line 811):

    const jtCap = K.muMax * Math.abs(jn) * K.jtCap * Math.max(aG.jtCap, bG.jtCap);

   with:

    // Bite has to raise the CEILING and not just the friction coefficient. On a
    // real chassis jt is already saturated against this cap by a factor of
    // eleven, so tripling `gear` on its own moves the spin transferred by
    // exactly zero. Measured on the attack against balance reference pair:
    // raw jt 0.368, cap 0.032. This is the same trap the Spin Thief rig fell
    // into and the reason the jtCap lever exists in RIG_NEUTRAL at all.
    const biting = (a.t < a.bite || b.t < b.bite) ? 2.2 : 1;
    const jtCap = K.muMax * Math.abs(jn) * K.jtCap * Math.max(aG.jtCap, bG.jtCap) * biting;

   Read site: collide, tangential section, immediately before jt is clamped.
   At the cap one hit moves about 68 rad/s of spin, so the lift is worth roughly
   another 82 rad/s per connected hit, about eight percent of launch spin.

abilities.fireCode:
// Plant and take it. Three seconds at a third of the incoming shock
// drain, bought with travel: drive drops to forty percent, so it cannot
// chase, cannot dash and cannot pressure anything while it is doing it.
// Note this protects SPIN and not BALANCE; the tip formula reads absorb
// and not taken, so a Stoneskin top still gets tilted by a big hit. That
// is deliberate, because it keeps Anchor a different answer.
a.stone = a.t + 3.0; break;

// Flip the sign of the RIM velocity only. The top keeps turning the way
// it was turning and keeps driving the way it was driving; what changes
// is which way the contact patch is scrubbing. An opposite spin matchup
// meshes into a scrub for two and a half seconds, and a mirror matchup
// meshes instead, which is the one way to turn the violent short
// matchup into a grind without giving up your own spin to reversal.
a.backspin = a.t + 2.5; break;

{
  // Convert a quarter of the spin into one shove. A shove is delivered
  // by the rim, so it needs the rims near each other; at full stretch
  // across the dish it would read as telekinesis, which fails the rule
  // that an ability has to be visible without a caption. Full strength
  // touching, nothing three centimetres past contact, and the spin is
  // only spent on what actually lands, so a pitch thrown at nobody costs
  // the round's one ability and not the round.
  const dx = a.x - b.x, dz = a.z - b.z, d = Math.hypot(dx, dz) || 1;
  const reach = clamp(1 - (d - (a.spec.R + b.spec.R)) / 0.030, 0, 1);
  if (reach > 0) {
    a.w -= Math.sign(a.w) * Math.abs(a.w) * 0.25 * reach;
    // Newton's third law is the whole risk. They go one way and you go
    // the other, and whichever of you was already nearer the lip is the
    // one who finds it.
    b.vx -= dx / d * 1.90 * reach; b.vz -= dz / d * 1.90 * reach;
    a.vx += dx / d * 0.55 * reach; a.vz += dz / d * 0.55 * reach;
    b.fx = 'shoved';
  }
}
break;

{
  // Whatever last landed on this top, fired straight back at the top
  // that brought it. The 'echo' guard is what stops two Echo cores in
  // the same dish recursing until the stack gives out.
  const back = a.lastHitBy;
  if (back && back !== 'echo') {
    a.spec = Object.assign({}, a.spec, { ability: back });
    a.abilityUsed = 0; a.charge = 1;
    fire(a, b);
    // Restore from the CURRENT spec, never from a copy taken before the
    // inner call. Reversal, Shed and Scatter all REPLACE a.spec, and
    // rebuilding from a saved copy silently throws their work away: an
    // echoed Reversal came out spinning one way with a dir field that
    // still said the other, so drive pushed against the rotation for the
    // rest of the round.
    a.spec = Object.assign({}, a.spec, { ability: 'echo' });
    a.abilityUsed = 1; a.charge = 0; a.fx = 'echo';
  }
}
break;

// Stage one starts now and ends at a.windUp; stage two runs for the two
// seconds after that. Read in stepTop through twoStage, which is the
// same helper Cold Start uses, so there is one pattern in the file for
// slow then fast and one place to get it wrong.
a.windUp = a.t + 1.5; break;

abilities.stepHook:
1. ADD this helper beside clamp() in the STATE section, after kill():

  /* A two stage timer: slow first, then fast, then back to normal. Cold Start
   * and Wind Up are the same shape with different numbers, so there is one
   * pattern to read. `at` is the moment the slow stage ends, `span` is how long
   * the fast stage lasts, and `at <= 0` means the timer was never armed. */
  function twoStage(t, at, slow, fast, span) {
    if (at <= 0) return 1;
    if (t < at) return slow;
    if (t < at + span) return fast;
    return 1;
  }

